    "ls|LS16_S16_U_2": " \n \n \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *2 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2018 \n \nHALLMARKING OF JEWELLERY \n \n *2. SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI: SHRIMATI SANTOSH AHLAWAT:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Government is planning to make hallmarking mandatory for \ngold jewellery sold in the country and if so, the details thereof along with the likely date of \nimplementation of the same;  \n(b)  the number of Bureau of Indian Standard s (BIS) –recognised assaying and hallmarking \ncentres presently in the country, State and UT-wise; and  \n(c)  whether the Government is working on increasing the number of BIS – recognised assaying \nand hallmarking centres in the country, if so, the number of c entres which are going to be \nopened in Rajasthan? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (c) :    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n \n   \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*2 FOR 11.12.2018 REGARDING HALLMARKING OF JEWELLERY. \n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \n(a)  : The Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) Act 2016 was notified on 22 nd March, 2016 and it came \ninto force w.e.f. 12 th October, 2017. BIS (Hallmarking) Regulations, 2018 framed under the BIS Act, \n2016 have been notified vide official gazette dated 14 th June, 2018.  Gold and Silver jewellery and \nartefacts have been notified as the precious metal articles to be marked with ‘Hallmark’ under  the BIS \nAct. \n \nThe BIS Act, 2016 has enabling provisions for making hallmarking of precious metal articles \nmandatory by the Central Government. However, Central Government is yet to take a decision in the \nmatter. \n \n(b)  : The State and UT -wise number of BIS -recognized Assaying and Hallmarking centres is at \nAnnexure.  \n \n(c)  : The setting up of an Assaying and Hallmarking centre is a market driven activity where decision \nto open an Assaying and Hallmarking centre is taken by the private entrepreneur depending upon the \ncommercial viability as assessed by the entrepreneur at that location. \n  \nBIS is also implementing a scheme namely Setting up of Gold Hallmarking/Assaying Centres in \nIndia with central assistance for places where no such centre is available. The ma in purpose of this \nscheme is to create infrastructure facilities in terms of Assaying and Hallmarking centres and Capacity \nbuilding by Training of artisans, Training of Trainers (BIS auditors) and Training of personnel of \nAssaying and Hallmarking centres. \n \nAt present, 37 Assaying and Hallmarking centres have been recognized by BIS in the State of \nRajasthan and one more centre is going to be opened in Jaipur, Rajasthan. \n \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART  (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*2 FOR 11.12.2018 REGARDING HALLMARKING OF JEWELLERY. \n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \nThe State and UT - wise number of the BIS-recognized Assaying and Hallmarking centres as \non 25 Oct 2018: \n \nSr. No. State/UT No. of  Assaying and Hallmarking centres \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 40 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 0 \n3.  Assam 2 \n4.  Bihar 18 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 6 \n6.  Goa 2 \n7.  Gujarat 75 \n8.  Haryana 16 \n9.  Himachal Pradesh 1 \n10.  Jammu and Kashmir 3 \n11.  Jharkhand 6 \n12.  Karnataka 41 \n13.  Kerala 60 \n14.  Madhya Pradesh 14 \n15.  Maharashtra 108 \n16.  Manipur 0 \n17.  Meghalaya 0 \n18.  Mizoram 0 \n19.  Nagaland 0 \n20.  Odisha 16 \n21.  Punjab 17 \n22.  Rajasthan 37 \n23.  Sikkim 0 \n24.  TamilNadu 74 \n25.  Telangana 21 \n26.  Tripura 1 \n27.  Uttar Pradesh 45 \n28.  Uttarakhand 1 \n29.  West Bengal 73 \n30.  Andaman & Nicobar Islands 0 \n31.  Chandigarh 3 \n32.  Delhi 39 \n33.  Dadra & Nagar Haveli 0 \n34.  Daman & Diu 0 \n35.  Puducherry 2 \n36.  Lakshadweep 0 \n Total 721 \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S16_U_20": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE,\n \nCOOPERATION\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n20\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \nTHE \n11\nTH\n \nDECEMBER\n, \n201\n8\n \n \nPRADHAN MANTRI FASAL BIMA YOJANA\n \n \n20. \n \nSHRI JAYADEV \nGALLA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n  \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe status of implementation of Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana in the country, \n  \nState\n-\nwise with a particular reference to Andhra Pradesh and Telangana; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether it has come to the notice of t\nhe Government that farmers, on an average, have \nto wait for 18 months for claims; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe extent to which delay in payment of premium by Union and State Governments is \ncontributing to delays in settling of claims; and \n \n \n(d)\n \n \nthe percentage of farmers wh\no are covered since implementation of the scheme, Rabi \nand Kharif season\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA)\n \n \n(a) to (d):\n \nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) has been under implementation in \nthe country including the States of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana since Kharif 2016 season.\n \n \n \nVarious measures namely, timelines for settlement of claims, leveraging of technology \nfor assessment of yield by State Government agencies \nusing smartphone\n/CCE Agri App, \nintegration of all individual farmer data on National Crop Insurance Portal etc. have\n \nbeen taken \nunder PMFBY.  However, due to use of manual process for \ntransmission\n \nyield data; late release \nof their share in premium subsidy by some States like Andhra Pradesh and Telangana; yield \nrelated disputes between insurance companies and States, non\n-\nreceipt \nof account details of \nsome farmers for transfer of claims etc. settlement of claims in some States has been delayed \nunder the scheme.\n \n \n \nTo resolve the issue of delay in payment of claims, timelines have been tightened and \npenalty\n \nprovision of 12%\n \ninterest rate per annum by Insurance Company to farmers for delay in \nsettlement claims beyond 10 days of prescribed cut\n-\noff date for payment of claims; 12% inte\nrest \nrate for delay in release of State share of Subsidy beyond three months of prescribed cut\n-\noff \ndate/submission of requisition by Insurance Companies etc. have been prescribed in the \nrevised Operational Guidelines issued recently.   \n \n \n \nSeason\n-\nwise/Year\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise details of farmer enrolled under the Pradhan \nMantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), since its inception from Kharif 2016 till Rabi 2017\n-\n18, are \ngiven in \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n*********\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S16_U_200": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.12.2018 \n \nWORKERS/LABOURERS RENDERED UNEMPLOYED \n*200. SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE:  \n          SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB: \n   \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND  EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a) whether it is a fact that a number of workers/labourers have \nbecome unemployed on account of closure or becoming sick of \nindustries/factories/establishments, retrenchment or permanent \ndisability across the country duri ng each of the last three years \nand the current year and if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;  \n(b) whether the Government has provided unemployment allowance or \nany sort of relief to such workers/labourers during the said period, \nif so, the details t hereof, State/UT -wise and if not, the reasons \ntherefor;  \n(c) the details of the schemes/ programmes being implemented by the \nGovernment to provide employment to such workers/labourers or \nrevive the closed/ sick industries/factories/establishments across \nthe country along with the number of such workers/labourers \nbenefited therein during the said period; and \n (d) the other steps taken by the Government in this direction? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***                                                   ******* \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 200 FOR ANSWER ON 24.12.2018 \nREGARDING WORKERS/LABOURERS RENDERED UNEMPLOYED  BY \nSHRI RAHUL SHEWALE AND SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB. \n(a): Labour Bureau collects & compiles statistics on Closure and \nRetrenchment by Industrial Establishments based on the voluntary \nreturns received every month from the Labour D epartments of the \nStates and the Regional Labour Commissioner (Central) under the \nIndustrial Disputes Act, 1947. The State/UT -wise information of \nClosure  and Retrenchment or the years 2015 to 2018  based on the \nreturns/clarifications received in the Labour  Bureau till 30th \nNovember, 2018 is at Annexure-I &II. \n(b) to (d):  The Government is implementing Counselling, Retraining & \nRedeployment (CRR) Scheme as a social safety net to  provide \nopportunities of self / wage employment to the employees (or \ndependents) of Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) separated \nunder VRS/V SS or retrenched due to closure / restructuring of CPSEs. \nCRR aims to reorient VRS/VSS optees/ dependents through short \nduration skill development training to enable them to adjust in the new \nenvironment and adopt new avocations after their separation from the \nCPSEs. CRR scheme is being implemented in collaboration w ith \nNational Skill Development C orporation (NSDC) under Ministry of Skill \nDevelopment & Entrepreneurship.  \n \n  \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nState/UT-wise number of Permanent Closures & Workers affected during the last 3 years and the \ncurrent year till 30.11.2018 (Provisional) \n  (Source: Labour Bureau) \nFor the Year 2018 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nNumber of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nRajasthan Nil Nil 1 118 \nTelangana Nil Nil 1 184 \nUttarakhand Nil Nil 1 8 \nTOTAL : Nil Nil 3 310 \nFor the Year 2017 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nNumber of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nAssam Nil Nil 1 109 \nHaryana Nil Nil 1 145 \nKerala Nil Nil 1 5 \nMadhya Pradesh 1 547 Nil Nil \nOdisha 1 429 Nil Nil \nTelangana Nil Nil 4 1003 \nTripura Nil Nil 5 29 \nUttar Pradesh Nil Nil 4 234 \nUttarakhand Nil Nil 2 188 \nTOTAL : 2 976 18 1713 \nFor the Year 2016 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of Units Number of \nWorkers \nAffected \nNumber of Units Number of Workers \nAffected \nHimachal Pradesh Nil Nil 3 204 \nOdisha Nil Nil 2 943 \nTripura Nil Nil 14 566 \nUttar Pradesh Nil Nil 6 345 \nUttarakhand Nil Nil 1 21 \nTOTAL : Nil Nil 26 2079 \nFor the Year 2015 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of Units Number of \nWorkers \nAffected \nNumber of Units Number of Workers \nAffected \nAndhra Pradesh Nil Nil 1 260 \nChhattisgarh 1 153 Nil Nil \nGoa Nil Nil 2 44 \nHimachal Pradesh Nil Nil 1 90 \nKarnataka Nil Nil 1 96 \nMaharashtra 1 12 Nil Nil \nOdisha Nil Nil 1 36 \nTripura Nil Nil 9 687 \nUttar Pradesh Nil Nil 4 118 \nTOTAL : 2 165 19 1331 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE-II \n \nState/UT-wise number of Retrenchments & Workers affected during the last 3 years and the \ncurrent year till 30.11.2018 (Provisional) \n     (Source: Labour Bureau) \n   For the Year 2018 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nNumber of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nKarnataka Nil Nil 1 9 \nTOTAL : Nil Nil 1 9 \n  For the Year 2017 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nNumber of \nUnits \nNumber of Workers \nAffected \nHimachal Pradesh Nil Nil 1 36 \nUttar Pradesh Nil Nil 2 50 \nTOTAL : Nil Nil 3 86 \nFor the Year 2016 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of Units Number of \nWorkers \nAffected \nNumber of Units Number of Workers \nAffected \nHimachal Pradesh Nil Nil 1 5 \nMaharashtra 2 66 Nil Nil \nOdisha 1 3559 Nil Nil \nTelangana Nil Nil 1 15 \nTOTAL : 3 3625 2 20 \nFor the Year 2015 \nSTATE / UNION TERRITORY Central Sphere State Sphere \n Number of Units Number of \nWorkers \nAffected \nNumber of Units Number of Workers \nAffected \nKerala Nil Nil 1 24 \nOdisha 10 422 Nil Nil \nRajasthan 1 87 Nil Nil \nTOTAL : 11 509 1 24 \n \n********* ",
    "ls|LS16_S16_U_201": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.201 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.12.2018 \n \nMULTI-PURPOSE STALL POLICY \n \n    *201. SHRI ARVIND SAWANT:  \n              SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO  GAWALI  PATIL:            \n \n \n                Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Multi -purpose Stall Policy (MPS) \nhas been framed and issued to the Zonal Railways;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n \n(c) whether it is also a fact that a number of existing bookstalls \nhave recently been converted into multi-purpose stalls across \nthe country including Mathura Junction under North Central \nrailway and if so, the details thereof, Division and Zone-wise;  \n \n(d) the de tails of stations where existing bookstalls can be \nconverted into multi -purpose stalls, Division and Zone -wise; \nand \n \n(e) the details of terms and conditions laid down for converting a \nbookstall into multi-purpose stall? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COAL \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a)  to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.201 BY SHRI ARVIND SAWANT AND SHRIMATI \nBHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI  PATIL TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 26.12.2018 REGARDING MULTI-PURPOSE STALL POLICY \n \n(a) & (b) : Yes, Madam. A  new policy on Multi Purpose Stalls (MPS) was \nissued on 05.09.2017, the salient features of which are; \n \n All Miscellaneous/curio stalls, bookstalls (other than philanthropic), \nchemist stalls/corners to come under MPS. \n \n  All miscellaneous items that are essential and incidental to the \ntravelling needs including artefacts & items of local and regional \nimportance, Packed  items as per the extant procedure of Zonal \nRailway, Packaged Drinking Water, books/magazines /newspapers, \nRailway Time Tables, chemist stall items - Over The Counter (OTC) \nmedicines and non -pharmacy items viz. dry/spray milk powder etc. \nare to be permitted for sale from Multi Purpose Stalls. \n \n A bluep rint of the total requirement of the MPS, platform/station \nwise for the whole Division, number of persons to be deployed on \neach MPS, etc. to be decided by DRMs with the approval of CCMs of \ntheir respective Zonal Railway. \n \n Retailers/Individuals/Self Help G roups (SHGs)/ registered sole \nproprietorship firm or a partnership firm/Company incorporated \nunder Partnership Act 1932/Companies Act 1956 to be eligible for \nallotment of MPS.   In addition, Aadhar Card, Government approved \nID proof and residence proof suc h as PAN/Voter ID to ensure \ngenuineness of the bidder and VAT registration, etc is required. \n\n \n Allotment of MPS at all stations is to be done by e -tendering method. \nTill the finalization of e -tendering module, normal tendering process \nis to be followed.  \n \n Earnest Money Deposit ( EMD) to be fixed as 10% of the Minimum \nReserve Price. Security Deposit shall be fixed as 10% of the quoted \nlicense fee. \n \n Allotment of all MPS to be made  for a period of only 5 years. There \nshall be no extension and renewal of the MPS units. \n \n Minimum Licence Fee is to be fixed as minimum 12 % of annual \nsales turnover calculated on the basis of Senior Administrative \nGrade (SAG) formula which takes into account various parameters. \n \n Zonal Railways to fix the area/space along with standardized design \nfor MPS prior to notification for allotment, taking into consideration \nthe number of trains and passengers, width of platform, location of \nthe unit on platform and availability of space to avoid inconvenience \nto passengers.  \n \n Reservation in allotment of MPS to be given to Scheduled Case, \nScheduled tribes, Other Backward Classes, Minorities, Divyang, \nFreedom Fighters/war widows and widows of railway employees, \npersons who have been dislocated/ displaced due to their land \nhaving been taken over by the railways for its own use and People \nBelow Poverty Line. There is provision of Sub -quota in both reserved \nand unreserved categories. Provision of 33% sub quota for women i n \nallotment of each of the reserved category and general category of \nMPS at all category of stations.  \n \n\n No firm/Company/Individual/Retailer is allowed to hold more than five \nMPS per Division. However, a firm/Company/Individual can hold a \nmaximum of 10 Multi Purpose Stalls over each Zonal Railway.   \n \n Applicability of the policy on the existing stalls: - No new allotment, \nrenewal, extension of the Bookstal l/table (other than philanthropic), \nMisc. Stalls/trolley, Chemist Stalls/corner etc. shall be done by the \nrailways. All such existing stalls/trolley etc. to be allowed for \nconversion into MPS subject to payment of license fee fixed as per \nnew MPS policy. T enure of such units will be for a period of 5 years \nfrom the date of conversion. In case they do not opt for conversion \ninto MPS, the same to be allowed to continue till expiry of the \nexisting agreement/arrangement in vogue. \n \n After expiry of the current agreement period, space shall be \nstandardized, identified and allotted by Zonal Railways for new MPS \nas per the extant procedure.  \n \n \n(c) & (d): Yes, Madam. As per information collected, 156 number of \nbookstalls have been convert ed into Multi Purpose Stalls over Indian \nRailways, including one at Mathura Junction of North Central Railway , \ndetails of which are Appended. As per current MPS Policy Para 11, all \nexisting Book Stalls (except Philanthropic) at stations have option to \nconvert to Multi Purpose Stalls.   \n \n(e): As per the new  Multi Purpose Stall Policy(MPS) effective from \n05.09.2017, (i) no new allotment, renewal, extension of the Book Stall \n(other than Philanthropic) shall be permitted. (ii) All such existing Book \nStalls shall be allowed for conversion into Multi Purpose Stalls as per the \nprovisions of the Multi Purpose  Stall Policy. (iii)  In case of not opting for \n\nconversion of existing Book Stall into Multi Purpose Stall the same may be \nallowed to continue till expiry of t he existing agreement/arrangement in \nvogue. (iv) After expiry of the current agreement period, space shall be \nstandardized, identified and allotted by Zonal Railways for new Multi \nPurpose Stall as per the extant procedure.  (v) The existing Bookstalls \n(other than philanthropic) shall be given an option to convert to Multi \nPurpose Stalls subject to payment of Licence Fee for a period of 5 years. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAPPENDIX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (c) AND (d) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 201 BY SHRI ARVIND SAWANT AND SHRIMATI BHAVANA \nPUNDALIKRAO GAWALI  PATIL TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON \n26.12.2018 REGARDING MULTI-PURPOSE STALL POLICY \n \n(c) & (d): Zonal Railway wise Status of Multi Purpose Stall (MPS)         \nConversion \nAs on 31.10.2018 \nZonal \nRailway Type of Units \nNo. of \nexisting \nUnits \nNo. of existing \nunits whose \nLicensees have \nopted for \nconversion into \nMPS  \nNo. of \nunits \nConverted \ninto MPS  \nCentral \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 138 30 5 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 47 17 11 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 1 1 1 \nEast \nCentral \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 143 120 18 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 28 25 6 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 0 0 0 \nEast Coast \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 52 10 0 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 3 3 3 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 3 1 1 \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nEastern \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 93 31 19 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 30 0 0 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 2 1 1 \nNorth \nCentral \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 84 14 4 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 5 5 4 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 0 0 0 \nNorth \nEastern \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 105 9 0 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 21 20 19 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 0 0 0 \nNortheast \nFrontier # \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 42 13 0 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 1 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 10 0 0 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 1 0 0 \nNorthern # \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 142 31 22 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 13 2 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 70 26 26 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 1 1 1 \n \n \n\nNorth \nWestern  \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 45 35 5 \nBook Stall cum chemist \ncorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 0 0 0 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 1 1 1 \nSouth \nCentral \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 103 14 3 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 9 8 7 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 2 0 1 \nSouth East \nCentral  \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 30 30 30 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 0 0 0 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 2 2 2 \nSouth \nEastern \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 43 43 0 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 16 16 0 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 0 0 0 \nSouthern \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 33 19 19 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 0 0 0 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 40 24 24 \nExclusive Chemist \nstalls/Corners 0 0 0 \n \n \n \n \n\nSouth \nWestern \nBook Stalls/ Trolleys/Table etc. 19 9 9 \nBook Stall cum Chemist \nCorners 1 1 1 \nMisc/Curio Stall/ Trolleys/ \nTable etc. 44 40